{"step_index":1111,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T23:31:15Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-12T01:31:15+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-12T01:31:15+02:00\nFile Path: `file:///mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_e20263a9/reference/GraphInvariantsComputable.lean`\nTotal Lines: 537\nTotal Bytes: 27292\nShowing lines 1 to 35\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: /-\n2: GraphInvariantsComputable.lean — a *computable* mirror of the invariants in\n3: `GraphInvariants.lean`, so that a claim about one explicit finite graph can be\n4: settled by `decide` rather than by a hand proof.\n5: \n6: Why this file exists\n7: --------------------\n8: `GraphInvariants.lean` defines the invariants the way a mathematician states\n9: them: `dominationNumber G = sInf { n | ∃ s, s.card = n ∧ G.IsDominatingSet s }`.\n10: That is the right *specification*, but `sInf` over a `Set ℕ` (under\n11: `open scoped Classical`) is **noncomputable**, so the kernel cannot evaluate it\n12: on a concrete graph — measured directly, `decide` succeeds on `order`, `size`\n13: and `minDegree`, and fails on every `sInf`/`sSup` invariant (domination,\n14: annihilation, the zero-forcing family, and mathlib's own `indepNum`/`cliqueNum`).\n15: \n16: That blocks the cheapest source of formal results we have. The refutation engine\n17: kills thousands of candidates and knows exactly which graph kills each one; each\n18: such pair is a `¬∀ …` theorem whose proof is \"evaluate both sides on this graph\"\n19: — but only if the invariants actually evaluate.\n20: \n21: The definitions here are the same mathematical objects expressed as a minimum\n22: over an explicitly enumerated `Finset`, which the kernel *can* reduce. They are\n23: deliberately kept in their own namespace: nothing in the existing proof pipeline\n24: changes, and `GraphInvariants.lean` remains the specification of record.\n25: -/\n26: import Mathlib\n27: \n28: open Finset\n29: \n30: namespace GraphCalc\n31: \n32: variable {V : Type*} [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V]\n33: \n34: /-- Least cardinality of a subset satisfying `p`, or `0` when none does.\n35: \nThe above content does NOT show the entire file contents. If you need to view any lines of the file which were not shown to complete your task, call this tool again to view those lines.\n"}
{"step_index":1112,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T23:31:15Z","thinking":"**Understanding Computable Graph Invariants**\n\nThe `GraphInvariantsComputable.lean` file provides computable versions of graph invariants. This means claims about specific finite graphs can be automatically verified. The refutation engine uses this file to efficiently identify graphs that disprove certain candidates.\n\n\n**Investigating Potential Counterexamples**\n\nExamining the potential for a counterexample to the inequality, previous testing revealed no instances up to n=7. Considering n>=8, the initial approach involves checking all connected graphs on n=8.\n\nRealizing the radius of a graph on n=8 is at most 7, and graphs with radius <=2 are already known to satisfy the inequality, it's necessary to only check graphs with radius >=3. This significantly reduces the search space. A Python search will use `PuLP` and `networkx` to exhaustively test these graphs.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/.venv/bin/python -u -c '\\nimport networkx as nx\\nimport itertools\\n\\n# We can generate all connected graphs on 8 vertices or search trees and low degree graphs\\n# Actually, networkx has geng or graph generators, or we can use random generation\\n# But wait! What graphs have radius >= 3 on n=8?\\n# Diam >= 3, up to 7.\\n# Let us search among all trees on n=8, 9, 10, 11, 12, 13, 14, 15, 16!\\nprint(\\\"Testing trees up to n=18...\\\")\\nfor n in range(8, 19):\\n    trees = list(nx.nonisomorphic_trees(n))\\n    for T in trees:\\n        r = nx.radius(T)\\n        if r <= 1: continue\\n        # compute roman domination of T using tree DP or pulp\\n        # Fast Roman domination for T\\n        import pulp\\n        prob = pulp.LpProblem(\\\"R\\\", pulp.LpMinimize)\\n        nodes = list(T.nodes())\\n        x = {v: pulp.LpVariable(f\\\"x_{v}\\\", cat=\\\"Binary\\\") for v in nodes}\\n        y = {v: pulp.LpVariable(f\\\"y_{v}\\\", cat=\\\"Binary\\\") for v in nodes}\\n        prob += pulp.lpSum(x[v] + 2*y[v] for v in nodes)\\n        for v in nodes:\\n            prob += x[v] + y[v] + pulp.lpSum(y[u] for u in T.neighbors(v)) >= 1\\n            prob += x[v] + y[v] <= 1\\n        prob.solve(pulp.PULP_CBC_CMD(msg=0))\\n        rom = int(pulp.value(prob.objective))\\n        if r + 1 > rom:\\n            print(f\\\"COUNTEREXAMPLE TREE! n={n}, r={r}, rom={rom}\\\")\\n            exit(0)\\n    print(f\\\"All {len(trees)} trees on n={n} passed.\\\")\\n\\nprint(\\\"All trees up to n=18 passed!\\\")\\n'\"","Cwd":"\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_e20263a9\"","WaitMsBeforeAsync":"5000","toolAction":"\"Testing all nonisomorphic trees up to n=18\"","toolSummary":"\"Test all trees up to n=18\""}}]}
{"step_index":1113,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"RUNNING","created_at":"2026-09-11T23:31:20Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-12T01:31:20+02:00\nTool is running as a background task with task id: f458c9cf-488d-4cd8-8365-7c4e4b16e3f9/task-1113\nTask Description: /mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/.venv/bin/python -u -c '\nimport networkx as nx\nimport itertools\n\n# We can generate all connected graphs on 8 vertices or search trees and low degree graphs\n# Actually, networkx has geng or graph generators, or we can use random generation\n# But wait! What graphs have radius >= 3 on n=8?\n# Diam >= 3, up to 7.\n# Let us search among all trees on n=8, 9, 10, 11, 12, 13, 14, 15, 16!\nprint(\"Testing trees up to n=18...\")\nfor n in range(8, 19):\n    trees = list(nx.nonisomorphic_trees(n))\n    for T in trees:\n        r = nx.radius(T)\n        if r <= 1: continue\n        # compute roman domination of T using tree DP or pulp\n        # Fast Roman domination for T\n        import pulp\n        prob = pulp.LpProblem(\"R\", pulp.LpMinimize)\n        nodes = list(T.nodes())\n        x = {v: pulp.LpVariable(f\"x_{v}\", cat=\"Binary\") for v in nodes}\n        y = {v: pulp.LpVariable(f\"y_{v}\", cat=\"Binary\") for v in nodes}\n        prob += pulp.lpSum(x[v] + 2*y[v] for v in nodes)\n        for v in nodes:\n            prob += x[v] + y[v] + pulp.lpSum(y[u] for u in T.neighbors(v)) >= 1\n            prob += x[v] + y[v] <= 1\n        prob.solve(pulp.PULP_CBC_CMD(msg=0))\n        rom = int(pulp.value(prob.objective))\n        if r + 1 > rom:\n            print(f\"COUNTEREXAMPLE TREE! n={n}, r={r}, rom={rom}\")\n            exit(0)\n    print(f\"All {len(trees)} trees on n={n} passed.\")\n\nprint(\"All trees up to n=18 passed!\")\n'\nTask logs are available at: file:///tmp/agyhome.NWyWnj/.gemini/antigravity-cli/brain/f458c9cf-488d-4cd8-8365-7c4e4b16e3f9/.system_generated/tasks/task-1113.log\nYOU MUST TAKE ONE OF THE FOLLOWING TWO ACTIONS: A) either proceed to other relevant work (if any) or, B) simply update the user with a short message (that you have launched the command and will wait for it to finish) and end the turn.\n DO NOTHING ELSE."}
